{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/HiQS-Labs/Needle-fork/pulls/16","id":4487630803,"node_id":"PR_kwDOUQg_3c8AAAABC3vP0w","html_url":"https://github.com/HiQS-Labs/Needle-fork/pull/16","diff_url":"https://github.com/HiQS-Labs/Needle-fork/pull/16.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/HiQS-Labs/Needle-fork/pull/16.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/HiQS-Labs/Needle-fork/issues/16","number":16,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"#2: a rule token in an argument position is not an invocation","user":{"login":"noelsaw1","id":56978803,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2OTc4ODAz","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/56978803?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1","html_url":"https://github.com/noelsaw1","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/noelsaw1/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Closes the third instance of this bug class with the conceptual fix #2 asked for, not a fourth special-case list.\n\n> **This PR has been failed twice by adversarial review and rewritten each time.** Current head `fad8657`. The numbers below describe *that* head; earlier comments describe superseded heads and are left in place as the record.\n\n## The inversion\n\nThe first two instances were fixed with lists of specific programs (`ARG_CONSUMERS`, `PKG_MANAGERS`). The defect is not that certain programs take data — it is that a **bare-name rule could match anywhere in a segment**. The default is inverted:\n\n> A bare-name rule matches only in **command position**. Everything after the command is data unless the program is known to take subcommands.\n\nArrived at only after three rounds, the governing principle is broader than that:\n\n> **Option values and quoted text are data everywhere** — inside the command region, *and* inside the rules that read operands.\n\nAn unlisted program is handled by the default: `stat`, `realpath`, `shasum`, `basename`, `gzip` appear nowhere in the fix and label correctly. **A test pins that they stay unenumerated in every table**, because the first attempt added them to `ARG_CONSUMERS` — checked *before* `command_region` — so its class control passed while testing nothing.\n\n## Verification at `fad8657`\n\n| Check | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `pytest -q -m \"not slow\"` | **244 passed**, 6 skipped, 6 deselected |\n| Regression tests added | 70 |\n| Red before green | 25 witnessed failing against the pre-fix module; all round-2 and round-3 findings reproduced as defects before being fixed |\n| The 14 instances reported in #2 | all re-labelled |\n| agy's findings (round 2) | 21 of 23 resolved; 2 documented as limitations |\n| agent2's escapes (round 3) | **5 of 5 resolved** |\n\nNew controls are **contrast pairs**: each real invocation must keep its label while an inert copy of the same trigger — quoted, or sitting in an option value — must not acquire it. A suite that only asserts `unmapped` can pass by labelling nothing.\n\n## Measured on 2,098 local Bash commands\n\n| Metric | Before | After |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Mapping coverage | 98.81% | **97.95%** |\n| Labels changed | — | **319 (15.2%)** |\n| Governance labels lost | — | **3**, each verified a false positive; **0 gained** |\n| `run_tests` | 22 | 14 |\n\nThe three governance losses: a relay-harness *locator* naming relay scripts in a candidate-path loop; a `gh issue create --title` mentioning `pdda`; and a heredoc writing a test file whose body contains the promotion pattern. All eight `run_tests` drops were adjudicated individually — six corrections, two tier artifacts on commands that both install and test.\n\n**Coverage falling is the intended direction only where the label was wrong.** An earlier version of this PR claimed the whole drop was false-positive elimination; that was false and is withdrawn.\n\n## What three rounds of review found\n\nRecorded because the pattern matters more than any single defect. Three regressions were caught only by diffing a re-extraction, six only by headless adversarial review, five more only by a second reviewer:\n\n1. Heredoc check hoisted above the ordered rules → 44 real commits became `run_script`.\n2. `\\d?>` treated `2>/dev/null` as a content write → 34 reads became writes.\n3. Segment reduction before the path rules hid a real `complete_doc` behind a `mkdir`.\n4. `--` treated as a flag → `git diff -- validate.sh` scored `run_validate`: **the defect #2 exists to remove, reintroduced by its own fix.**\n5. The class-level control was decorative — the programs it named were enumerated in the same diff.\n6. Blanking quotes before tokenising destroyed quoted operands, dropping real test runs.\n7. A flag's argument stayed in the region as command text → `git -C /tmp/validate.sh status` scored `run_validate`.\n8. The operand-reading rules searched raw text → `echo \"mv PROJECT/1-INBOX/…\"` scored `promote_capture`.\n\nRule *precedence* is load-bearing: the fix changes only the haystack each rule sees, never the order.\n\n## Known limitations — stated, not papered over\n\n- **Unlisted task runners degrade to `unmapped`** (`just test`, `rake test`, `bundle exec rspec`, `tox -e py311`). Safe direction, but a coverage gap, not a correction.\n- **The Studio corpus has not been re-extracted** — #2 requires it; the SMB share is not mounted here. The numbers in `TESTS-RESULTS/2026-09-07-taxonomy-v1-studio/` are **stale, and *not* superseded by measurement**. This PR's receipt supersedes nothing there: it measures local transcripts only. **Re-extract before cutting `label_set_version` v1.0.0.**\n- `oracle/labels-v1.json` unchanged: no label's *meaning* moved, only which commands map to it.\n- **CodeRabbit has not reviewed this PR.** Its free-tier quota was consumed by #15 and it skips drafts; a green CodeRabbit *status check* here reflects the rate-limit comment, not a review.\n\nReceipt: `TESTS-RESULTS/2026-09-09-taxonomy-positional-fix/SUMMARY.md`. Aggregates only — the corpus is private and this repo is public.\n\nRefs #2, #1.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **Bug Fixes**\n  - Improved command classification so bare names are recognized only when used as commands, not as arguments, quoted text, option values, or heredoc content.\n  - Improved handling of wrappers, subcommands, global flags, redirections, heredocs, and inline interpreters.\n  - Corrected labels for package installation, test execution, Git workflows, and system inspection commands.\n  - Reduced false-positive labels while preserving valid command detections.\n\n- **Tests**\n  - Added extensive regression coverage for positional matching and command parsing edge cases.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->","created_at":"2026-09-09T17:26:39Z","updated_at":"2026-09-09T20:11:18Z","closed_at":"2026-09-09T19:16:47Z","merged_at":"2026-09-09T19:16:47Z","merge_commit_sha":"5ed316d380fcbde8d9fb3f2d6d78803af6cc45bc","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/HiQS-Labs/Needle-fork/pulls/16/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/HiQS-Labs/Needle-fork/pulls/16/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/HiQS-Labs/Needle-